<Base title="The Kolbes"
      eyebrow="Württemberg · Soho · the Nautilus · the Karoo"
      dek="A German tailor's family in Bond Street, a boy married at sixteen and a settler at seventeen, a missionary's circuit through the Orange Free State, and a Karoo farm named for a country its owner had never seen.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>George Augustus Kolbe, 1802–1844 — an 1820 Settler</h2>
  <p class="dek">The family's research notes wonder whether John Augustus Barry was born at sea with the
    1820 Settlers. He was not. But the family <em>does</em> have a documented settler, on the other side of
    that marriage, and his record is unusually complete.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem">Born</td><td>7 December 1802, <strong>Westminster St Anne, London</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Christened</td><td><strong>2 February 1803, St Anne's, Soho</strong> — the register names his parents <strong>John Gottlob &amp; Mary</strong>, and gives his birth as <strong>7 Dec 1802</strong>, matching the Burgersdorp gravestone to the day. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Married</td><td><strong>20 January 1819, St Leonard's, Shoreditch</strong> — Margaret Georgina Elizabeth <strong>Downing</strong>, <strong>on a sworn oath that both were "of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards"</strong>. He was sixteen; she was fifteen. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The allegation &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sailed</td><td><strong>Gravesend, 3 December 1819</strong>, on the ship <strong><em>Nautilus</em></strong>, in <strong>Thomas Owen's party</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Landed</td><td><strong>Algoa Bay, 14 April 1820</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trades</td><td><strong>Reverend Missionary, London Missionary Society</strong> · <strong>Chemist</strong> · <strong>Tailor</strong> — but his death notice calls him simply an <strong>Agriculturist</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td>1 December 1844, the farm <strong>"Wurtemburg"</strong>, field-cornetcy of Rhenosterberg, Knapdaar, district Colesberg</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buried</td><td>December 1844, <strong>Burgersdorp Old Cemetery</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Estate</td><td class="m"><strong>7698</strong> — KAB MOOC 6/9/35 (1845); <strong>read</strong>, and valued at <strong>about £1000 sterling</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">He was <b>sixteen</b> when he married at Shoreditch and <b>seventeen</b> when
    he stepped ashore at Algoa Bay. His wife was about <b>fifteen</b>.</p>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:18px">
    And they said otherwise. The <b>marriage allegation of 20 January 1819</b> has him swear on oath that
    he is <strong>"a Bachelor of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards"</strong> and she
    <strong>"a Spinster of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards."</strong> Under Hardwicke's Marriage
    Act a minor could not marry by licence without a parent's consent. <b>Both ages are false.</b>
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The document &rarr;</a>
  </blockquote>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    The burial record carries a note in Dutch: <em>"Zijn vrouw is later bijgezet in het graf"</em> —
    <b>"his wife was later laid in the same grave."</b> She outlived him by twenty-three years and was
    buried beside him at Burgersdorp. <b>The stone is still standing</b>, and it names them both.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The grave &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0;max-width:420px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-george-painted-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Painted portrait of a young man with reddish-brown curling hair, high white stock and tan coat." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong> — a <strong>painting</strong>,
      photographed under glass, in the dress of the 1820s–30s. He died in 1844, when photography was five
      years old; a likeness of him could only ever have been painted.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The portraits &rarr;</a></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0;max-width:640px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-grave-inscription.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Cut inscription on the Kolbe monument at Burgersdorp naming George Augustus Kolbe and Margaret Downing." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"SACRED TO THE MEMORY OF <strong>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</strong>, BORN IN
      LONDON 7 DECEMBER 1802, AND DIED AT HIS FARM <strong>WURTHEMBURG</strong>, DIVISION OF ALBERT,
      1 DECEMBER 1844. ALSO HIS RELICT <strong>MARGARET DOWNING</strong>, BORN IN LONDON
      <strong>23 FEBRUARY 1803</strong>, AND DIED AT BURGHERSDORP <strong>2_ NOVEMBER 1867</strong>."
      Burgersdorp Old Cemetery. <b>This caption used to stop at her name.</b> The two lines under it
      give her an exact birth, where this archive carried "c. 1804", and settle a death date a newspaper
      had put in doubt. The day is a 2 and a digit that will not resolve.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The voyage, confirmed at last — and they lied about their ages again</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive has carried the <em>Nautilus</em> since the first day, entirely on the
    family tree's word. It has now been checked against the settler roll.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Confirmed</h3>
      <p><b>Owen's party</b> sailed from <b>Gravesend on 3 December 1819</b> in the <b>Nautilus</b>,
        reached <b>Table Bay on 17 March 1820</b> and <b>Algoa Bay on 14 April 1820</b>. Fifty-two
        settlers.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Every date the tree gave is right.
        <span class="chip doc">Open question 9, answered</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And a trade nobody had</h3>
      <p>The roll enters him as:</p>
      <p class="m" style="margin-top:8px;font-size:15px">KOLBE, George Augustus 20. <strong>Chemist</strong>. w Margaret 19.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">This archive has him as a <b>preacher</b> and a <b>farmer</b>. On the
        ship's list, at seventeen, he is a <b>chemist</b> — which is to say an apprentice apothecary, and
        a London tailor's son reaching for a profession.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Now put the ages beside the documents.</strong></p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Document</th><th class="m">He is stated to be</th><th class="m">He actually was</th><th class="m">She is stated</th><th class="m">She actually was</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>1819</b> — marriage allegation, Shoreditch</td><td class="m">21</td><td class="m"><b>16</b></td><td class="m">21</td><td class="m"><b>15</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>3 Dec 1819</b> — the <em>Nautilus</em> settler roll</td><td class="m">20</td><td class="m"><b>16</b></td><td class="m">19</td><td class="m"><b>16</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin:12px 0 0">He was born <b>7 December 1802</b> — the parish register says so — and the
      ship sailed four days before his seventeenth birthday. She was born <b>23 February 1803</b>, which
      her own death notice gives to the day.</p>
    <p style="margin:10px 0 0"><strong>They overstated their ages twice in one year, on two different
      official documents, to two different authorities.</strong> The archive already knew about the
      marriage allegation and treated it as a single desperate act by a couple who wanted to marry. It was
      not single. Within months they did it again — to get on a ship.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px">
    <span class="chip inf">What it does not tell us</span> Whether the overstatement was theirs or the
    party's. Thomas Owen had to present a party the Colonial Department would accept, and a
    seventeen-year-old chemist with a sixteen-year-old wife is a less convincing settler than a man of
    twenty with a trade. The lie may have been written for them. Either way it was on the paper.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The marriage allegation &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:14px">Settler roll: 1820settlers.com, Owen's party, accessed
    10 September 2026 — the same database whose citation for James Jonathan Mountjoy this archive found
    to be wrong, so it is used here for what it is: a compiled transcript, corroborating dates the tree
    already carried, and adding one occupation.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The children are a missionary's itinerary</h2>
  <p class="dek">Not a settler farm but a circuit — and two of the stops are London Missionary Society
    stations north of the Orange River.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Child</th><th>Born</th><th>Place</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{kids.map(([n, b, p, x]) => (
        <tr><td>{n}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td>{p}</td><td class="cap">{x}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The forenames are extraordinary — <b>Hachilah Machilah</b>, <b>Gerson Eliezar</b>, <b>Casimir</b>,
      <b>Fortunatus</b>. They read exactly as a missionary's children's names should: scriptural, Latinate,
      chosen rather than inherited.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>20 September 2026 &mdash; the itinerary was right, and a historian had written it
      down in 1992.</b> This section deduced a missionary circuit from a list of birthplaces, with no
      source for any of it. A draft <b>Dictionary of South African Biography</b> entry by
      <b>Karel Schoeman</b>, sent from Cape Town with the 1830 letter, gives the posting history from the
      Society&rsquo;s own records &mdash; and the two agree at <b>four separate points</b>:
      <b>Graaff-Reinet from about 1824</b> (three children born there, 1825&ndash;1829),
      <b>Philippolis from March 1831</b> (Harriet Hachilah Machilah born there <em>that year</em>),
      <b>Bethulie</b> after 1837 (Frederik Fortunatus, 1839, and Johan Casimir, 1844), and
      <b>Colesberg</b> at the end (Keetor Peter Benjamin, 1840). <b>Two lists made from different things,
      a hundred and sixty years apart, describing one man&rsquo;s movements.</b>
      <a href={u("/documents/#dsab-kolbe")}>The draft &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>And one of them travels. <b>Frederik Fortunatus Kolbe (1839)</b> becomes
      <b>Frederick Fortunatus Barry (1863)</b> becomes <b>Frederik Fortsenatus Booÿsen (1899)</b> — one
      name carried across three surnames and sixty years, degrading slightly at each handover.
      <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk" id="lms">
  <h2>He was a missionary &mdash; and the word now has a life attached to it</h2>
  <p class="dek">On 15 September this page worked out that he was a missionary from where his children
    were born, and found a library catalogue in Cape Town that had been saying so in four words since
    1985. On <b>18 September</b> the library sent the papers.</p>
  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p class="m" style="font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.9"><b>National Library of South Africa &middot;
      MSB 751 &mdash; the GA KOLBE COLLECTION</b><br />
      Donated by Dr Jean Griffiths, Somerset West, 1985. Photocopy, 4 pages.<br /><br />
      &ldquo;George Augustus Kolbe, <b>pharmacist</b>, 1820 settler, <b>missionary</b>, married Margaret
      Downing. + Letter, 1830 Aug. 23, from Johan Kolbe, London, to his son, George Augustus Kolbe
      <b>on his ordination</b>.&rdquo;</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>Three separate things line up and none of them knew about the others.</b> This page deduced a
      missionary circuit from a list of birthplaces. A memoir printed at Bloemfontein in 1869 &mdash;
      John Montgomery, founder of Burgersdorp, recalling a journey of about 1824 &mdash; puts him at
      <b>a Bushman mission school at Koolskop, where Colesberg now stands</b>. And a catalogue entry
      calls him an <b>LMS missionary</b>.</p>
    <p>It also reconciles the occupations, which had never agreed. The 1820 settler roll enters him as a
      <b>CHEMIST</b> aged twenty when he was seventeen. The catalogue says <b>pharmacist</b>. The 1844
      obituary describes <b>a medical practice</b>. <b>They are one trade, and the mission work is the
      part nobody had.</b></p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:20px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The letter came, and it is the first thing his father wrote that survives</h3>
    <p>Four pages, headed <b>&ldquo;London August 23. 1830&rdquo;</b>, opening <b>&ldquo;Dear
      George,&rdquo;</b> and signed <b>&ldquo;Your affectionate father, J G Kolbe&rdquo;</b>. This archive
      spent a fortnight trying to join the tailor of Conduit Street to the settler of Colesberg. <b>It now
      has them in one document, in the tailor&rsquo;s own hand.</b></p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>And it does not carry a street address.</b> The heading is
      &ldquo;London&rdquo; and nothing finer, so <b>Brunswick Square against Conduit Street is still
      open</b> &mdash; which was the single question the enquiry most wanted answered.
      <a href={u("/documents/#msb751")}>The letter &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p><b>And the catalogue was wrong about what kind of letter it is, and this page repeated it.</b>
      MSB 751 is described as written <em>&ldquo;on his ordination&rdquo;</em>, and this section said in
      a blockquote that he was <b>&ldquo;ordained, in or about August 1830&rdquo;</b>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>He was not.</b> The Dictionary of South African Biography draft dates
      the ordination to <b>25 May 1833</b>, by the French missionaries <b>Arbousset</b> and
      <b>Casalis</b> &mdash; and the letter itself says only that he had been <em>&ldquo;fully accepted by
      the [L.M.S.] as one of their Missionaries&rdquo;</em>, which is a different step. <b>There were
      three steps and this page had collapsed them into one:</b></p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="m" style="width:10rem">8 Feb 1828</td><td>accepted by the LMS as a <b>mission assistant</b>, and posted across the Orange</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">Aug 1830</td><td>accepted <b>as a missionary</b> &mdash; the news his father is writing about</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="m">25 May 1833</td><td><b>ordained</b>, at Philippolis, by Arbousset and Casalis</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>A catalogue description is a finding aid, not the document.</b> This
      archive quoted one as though it were the second.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Corrections &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk" id="mission-years">
  <h2>The mission years, 1824&ndash;1837 &mdash; on one source, and it is not a witness</h2>
  <p class="dek">Everything in this section comes from <b>a single secondary work</b>: Karel
    Schoeman&rsquo;s draft DSAB entry of February 1992. <b>This archive does not take secondary works as
    evidence</b>, and none of what follows has been tested against the Society&rsquo;s own papers. It is
    set down because it is the only account of these years anybody has ever handed this family, and
    because the parts of it that <em>can</em> be checked have checked out.
    <a href={u("/documents/#dsab-kolbe")}>How it was tested &rarr;</a></p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:20px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:11rem">When</th><th>What the entry says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="m">c. 1824</td><td>Enters the service of the <b>Graaff-Reinetse Zendelings Genootschap</b> and works for several years <b>among the slaves of the Graaff-Reinet district</b>, until the society can no longer afford him and merges with the LMS.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">8 Feb 1828</td><td>Accepted as a <b>mission assistant</b> and placed at the new station <b>Boesmanskool &mdash; the present Bethulie</b> &mdash; across the Orange, with <b>James Clark</b>, among the <b>San</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Nov 1828</td><td>Travels with <b>John Melvill</b> to the <b>Caledon River valley</b>, apparently as far as present-day <b>Ladybrand</b>: <b>the earliest journey in the present Free State of which a written record survives</b> &mdash; and <b>he wrote it</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Mar 1831</td><td>Transferred to the Griqua station <b>Philippolis</b>, with Melvill, and <b>alone</b> after Melvill leaves in March 1832.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">25 May 1833</td><td><b>Ordained</b> by <b>T. Arbousset</b> and <b>E. Casalis</b> of the Paris mission.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1833&ndash;34</td><td>Supports the French missionaries beginning work among <b>Moshweshwe&rsquo;s</b> Basotho, and advises the <b>Berlin Missionary Society</b> to found <b>Bethanie</b> for the Korana of the Riet River.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1836</td><td><b>Accused of adultery</b> with the wife of one of his congregation. <b>Acquitted after an inquiry by the French missionaries.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">Mar 1837</td><td>Withdraws from Philippolis. <b>Dismissed by the LMS on 19 June 1837.</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="m">1837&ndash;44</td><td>His farm <b>Driefontein</b> near Philippolis, then <b>Bethulie</b>, then <b>Wurtemberg</b> in the district of Colesberg, where he dies suddenly seven years later.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="panel" style="margin-top:22px">
    <h3 style="margin-top:0">The accusation, and why it is printed here in full</h3>
    <p>At Philippolis the station was split between Griqua and Baster, his predecessor Melvill had
      already been a casualty of it, and his own marriage was under strain. <b>In 1836 he was accused of
      adultery with the wife of one of his congregation</b> &mdash; a charge, the entry says,
      <em>&ldquo;often used as a means of revenge against missionaries of the period&rdquo;</em>.
      <b>H. Hendricks</b>, secretary of the Griqua Council, led the agitation. <b>An inquiry by the
      neighbouring French missionaries acquitted him.</b> His younger colleague <b>T. Atkinson</b> went
      on opposing him, and he left in March 1837.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:14px">This family did not know any of it. <b>It is one sentence in an unsigned
      draft, about a man who cannot answer</b>, and it would have been easy to keep the founding of
      Bethanie and lose this. <b>Keeping one and dropping the other is the worst thing an archive can do
      with a source.</b> He was accused, he was investigated, and <b>he was cleared</b>. All three belong
      on the page. <span class="chip inf">one source, untested</span></p>
    <p class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">The papers that would test it are the Griqua Council&rsquo;s
      own, and they are printed: <b>Schoeman (ed.), <i>Griqua Records: the Philippolis Captaincy,
      1825&ndash;1861</i></b>, Van Riebeeck Society II/25 &mdash; published two years after this
      typescript was typed, which is why its own author could not cite it there.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">He wrote something else, and it is findable</h3>
      <p>The November 1828 journey was <b>printed</b> &mdash; but under the other man&rsquo;s name, which
        is why searching for Kolbe found nothing: <span class="m">&ldquo;Extracts of the Journal of
        Messrs Melvill and Kolbe, addressed to the Rev. Richard Miles&hellip;, Philippolis, 25 Nov.
        1828&rdquo;</span>, in the <b>Council for World Missions archive</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Legassick quotes it. On the Caledon they met people with <b>&ldquo;no
        other means of subsistence than the wild animals found in these parts&rdquo;</b>. <b>The first
        words of his this archive has read, and they are about famine.</b></p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">The journey is printed entire in <b>Schoeman (ed.),
        <i>Early White Travellers in the Transgariep, 1819&ndash;1840</i></b> (Protea, 2003), p. 30.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0;font-size:15px">And there may be a face</h3>
      <p>This archive holds <b>no likeness of anybody born before 1850</b>. The entry names two: <b>a
        portrait of him as a young man</b>, in 1992 with a descendant at Ruigtevlei near Bethulie; and a
        <b>watercolour by Charles Bell of a church service at Philippolis in 1834, with Kolbe in the
        pulpit</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The second is <b>catalogued in Scotland</b> &mdash; University of
        Edinburgh, <span class="m">CSWC 60</span>, item 3, <b>&ldquo;Mr. Kolbe&hellip; at Philippolis in
        1834&rdquo;</b>. Edinburgh calls him <em>&ldquo;a moravian missionary?&rdquo;</em>, question mark
        and all. <b>He was LMS, and this archive has his father&rsquo;s letter saying so.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk" id="north">
  <h2>Then the family goes north</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Settler's tenth child had eight children of his own, and the register that names
    them was found by asking the index for a father rather than for a person. Three of the eight end up
    four hundred miles away, in the eastern Transvaal.</p>

  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>Frederik Fortunatus Kolbe</b>, born at the Bethulie mission station in
    1839, married <b>Elizabeth Johanna du Toit</b>. This archive did not know she existed until
    14 September 2026. She is named at eight fonts across eighteen years, and her name holds steady at
    every one of them but Hopetown, where the clerk wrote <i>Geertruida</i>.</p>

  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">Child</th><th>Born</th><th>Baptised at</th><th>On</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{north.map(([n, b, pl, d, x]) => (
        <tr><td>{n}</td><td class="m">{b}</td><td>{pl}</td><td class="m">{d}</td><td class="cap">{x}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The eldest is another <b>George Augustus</b>, named for a grandfather who had been dead nineteen
      years. He is baptised at Colesberg on 11 October 1863 &mdash; and he dies at
      <b>Wakkerstroom</b>, in the eastern Transvaal, on 28 September 1954, <b>aged ninety-one</b>. The
      age counts exactly from the font. In between he married <b>Martha Sophia Klopper</b>, who was
      seventeen; their eldest was born at Wakkerstroom in 1886; and he was dead at ninety-one before this
      archive existed.</p>

    <p class="note"><b>A prisoner of war, and this archive nearly gave him to the wrong man.</b>
      <span class="m">WO 108/368</span>, the master list of Boer prisoners numbers 1&ndash;32561, holds a
      <b>George Augustus Kolbe, aged 40, nationality Transvaaler, residence Wakkerstroom, prison
      Natal, 1902</b>. The residence pointed straight at this man and for about an hour this page said
      so. <b>The age does not fit him.</b> Born 29 July 1863, he was thirty-nine in 1902, not forty
      &mdash; and a <em>second</em> George Augustus Kolbe, born at Burgersdorp in 1861 and dead at
      Standerton in 1934, was forty or forty-one that year, and is himself recorded resident at
      Wakkerstroom on his marriage to Susanna Margaretha Swart. Two men of one name in one town.
      <b>The prisoner is more probably the older one</b>, and this archive is not going to decide it on
      an index alone. <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>

    <p>Two of his sisters are at Wakkerstroom too. <b>Margaret Harriet</b>, baptised at Hopetown in
      1870, marries Dirk Jacobus Aspheling van der Spuy there at twenty-four. <b>Elizabeth Johanna</b>,
      baptised at Colesberg in 1876, marries John Charles Pattison there at twenty-one. In both cases
      the age on the marriage agrees with the font to the year.</p>

    <p class="note"><b>This is the second proof of the Wakkerstroom connection, and it is the better
      one.</b> The first was a name: <b>Frederik Fortunatus <i>Downing</i> Kolbe</b>, born at
      Wakkerstroom in 1891, carrying a London surname that has no business in the eastern Transvaal
      except through a Shoreditch wedding of 1819. That was decisive but it was a single word on a
      single record. This is a <em>household</em> &mdash; a father, a mother, eight fonts in three
      towns, and three of the children turning up together at the far end. Names can be coincidence.
      A family moving is not.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">1918, four months apart</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">
        <b>Daniel Jakobus Kolbe</b>, born 3 May 1893, died <b>19 June 1918</b> at Wakkerstroom, aged 25.<br />
        <b>Frederik Fortunatus Downing Kolbe</b>, born 1891, died <b>2 November 1918</b> at Wakkerstroom, aged 27.
      </p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Two brothers, four months apart, in the year of the
        influenza. Their father outlived them both by thirty-six years. Daniel's death registration
        carries the words <b class="m">G A Kolbe's son</b>, which the index mangled into
        <b class="m">G A Kolbelsson</b> &mdash; and that misprint is the line that ties the graves to
        the old man.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Two women the war took, and one this archive cannot name</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">
        <b>Angeline Kolbe</b>, aged 28, died at <b>Wakkerstroom on 15 November 1900</b>.<br />
        <b>Martha Angeline Kolbe</b>, born 9 October 1901, died 8 December 1901, <b>buried at
        Volksrust</b> &mdash; sixty days old.
      </p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Volksrust held a concentration camp, and this archive
        records the burial place without claiming the cause, because it has read no camp register that
        names the child. <b>Angeline is not the camp grave NAAIRS holds</b> &mdash; that is a
        <b class="m">G.E. Kolbe, aged 35</b>, a different age in a different year in a different town.
        Who Angeline was, this archive does not know.
        <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>John Gottlob Kolbe, 1765–1845 — a tailor of Bond Street</h2>
  <p class="dek">The tree leads with the German original, <em>Johan Gottlieb</em>. Four documents — his son's
    baptism register, his own will, his death certificate and his burial entry — all call him
    <strong>John Gottlob</strong>. That is the name he used in England, and the one this archive follows.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem">Born</td><td>1765, <strong>Württemberg, Germany</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trade</td><td><strong>Tailor</strong>, of <strong>34 Conduit Street, Bond Street, Middlesex</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>His will</td><td>written of <em class="m">"JOHN GOTTLOB KOLBE of Conduit Street … in the county of Middlesex Tailor"</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Married</td><td>1793, London — <strong>Mary Larman</strong> (1768–1814)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>His will</strong></td><td>signed <strong>25 May 1842</strong>, proved at London <strong>23 Feb 1846</strong> in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury. A <strong>partnership business of Tailors</strong> with <strong>Richard Davies</strong>; his daughter <strong>Marian Kolbe</strong> named <strong>Executrix</strong>. <a href={u("/documents/")}>Read it &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td>18 November 1845, at <strong>34 <em>Conduit</em> Street, Mayfair</strong> — cause: <strong>"Decay of Nature"</strong>. <em>Not</em> Lamb's Conduit Street, as the tree has it. <a href={u("/documents/")}>Three documents say so &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Buried</td><td><strong>26 November 1845, the GERMAN SAVOY CHURCH (St John Baptist), the Strand, London</strong> — Grave 7, by Revd Dr Steinkopff. <em>Not</em> Leamington, Warwickshire. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The register &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">He appears under <b>three versions of his own name</b> — <em>Johan
    Gottlieb</em>, <em>John Gottlob</em>, <em>John Paul</em> — but the documents are consistent: every one
    of the four read by this archive says <b>John Gottlob</b>. That is what assimilation looks like in a
    parish register. His eldest daughter married <b>George Kottlich Kohlbeck</b> at St Anne's Soho in 1811,
    which suggests the family's German original was nearer <b>Kohlbeck</b> than <b>Kolbe</b>.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>A firm, not a jobbing tailor</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>His will, proved in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury in 1846, shows a business with capital and a
      partner — <b>Mr Richard Davies</b> — and directs that his money <b>stay invested in the partnership
      for fourteen years</b> after his death. Trustees were <b>Timothy Duke</b>, a gentleman of Lombard
      Street, <b>Christian William Kent</b>, a fellow tailor of Poland Street, and his own daughter
      <b>Marian Kolbe</b>, whom he appointed <b>Executrix</b> and who was sworn at probate as
      <em>"Marian Kolbe Spinster the daughter."</em></p>
    <p>For those fourteen years the profits went to such of his daughters <b>"as shall continue sole and
      unmarried"</b> — and any daughter's share <b>ceased the moment she married</b>, passing to her
      unmarried sisters. Provision and pressure in the same sentence.</p>
    <p>Then the residue divided <b>equally among all his children living at his death</b>, with
      <b>the issue of any child already dead taking that child's share</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <b>George Augustus Kolbe died in December 1844; his father in November 1845.</b> Under that clause
      George's share fell to <b>his own children — at Burgersdorp, in the Cape Colony</b>.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> There is, on paper, <b>a line of money from a Bond Street
      tailor's estate to the Barkly East family</b>. Whether any of it arrived is untested.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The will &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>German by burial, English by baptism</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The living children were christened at <b>St Anne's, Soho</b> — the Anglican parish. The dead were
      not.</p>
    <p>At least five of them were buried at <b>"St Marien's Children's Churchyard, The Strand"</b> — the
      burial ground of the <b>German Lutheran church</b> in the Strand. <b>Charles Samuel</b> (1806–1807),
      <b>Edward</b> (born and dead on 15 November 1808), <b>Frederick Charles</b> (d. 1809),
      <b>Frederica Carolina</b> (1813–1814) and <b>John Henry</b> (d. 1832) are all recorded there — and so
      is their mother <b>Mary Larman</b>, who died at St Marien's on 2 August 1814.</p>
    <p>A family standing in both communities at once: Anglican for the living, German for the dead.</p>
    <p><b>And German for the patriarch too.</b> The tree had him buried at <em>St John the Baptist,
      Leamington, Warwickshire</em>. The register says <b>"St John Baptist, in the German Savoy Church,
      in the County of Middlesex"</b> — the same Strand congregation, a hundred miles from Warwickshire,
      the ceremony performed by <b>Revd Dr Steinkopff</b>. He was buried with his wife's people.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The correction &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The detail that ties the two continents together</h2>
  <blockquote>
    A tailor's son from Bond Street sailed at seventeen, preached across the Orange River, and died in 1844
    on a Karoo farm he had named <strong>Wurtemburg</strong> — after the German country his father left and
    he had never seen.
  </blockquote>
  <p>He died eleven months before his own father, who was still alive above the shop in <strong>Conduit
    Street, Mayfair</strong>. Under the old man's will, George's share of the estate fell to
    <strong>his own children, at Burgersdorp</strong>. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The will &rarr;</a></p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And within seventy years he was gone.</b> In 1915 his own grandson, filling in the death notice of
    George's daughter Margaretha, wrote: <b>"Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name.
    Mother: Unknown."</b><br /><br />
    The gravestone at Burgersdorp was standing about forty miles away, with both their names on it.
    <a href={u("/documents/")}>The notice &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The line goes back further than the tree view shows</h2>
  <p class="dek">Retrieved from the tree's own card data. <b>Kolbe reaches 1699 and Downing 1683</b> —
    four generations deeper than this archive previously recorded. None of it is yet tested against a
    document.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Kolbe — Württemberg to London</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">
        <b>Johann Gottlieb Kolbe</b> (1699 – 29 Jan 1775) m. <b>Maria Barbara Jung</b> (c. 1700)<br />
        → <b>Johann Micheal "John" Kolbe</b> (5 Oct 1736 – 1780)<br />
        → <b>Johan Gottlieb "John Paul" Kolbe</b> (1765–1845) m. <b>Mary Larman</b> (1768–1814)<br />
        → <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> (1802–1844)
      </p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">Larman: <b>Thomas Hall Larman</b> (c. 1740 – c. 1800).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Downing — and three Devon surnames</h3>
      <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.8">
        <b>Edward Downing</b> (1700–1792) m. <b>Mary Heard</b> (b. 1702)<br />
        <b>John Trick</b> (b. 1683) m. <b>Margaret Hooper</b> (1691–1741)<br />
        → <b>Thomas Downing</b> (1736–1801) m. <b>Mary Trick</b> (b. 1731)<br />
        → <b>John Paul Downing</b> (1769–1839) m. <b>Mary P King</b> (1768–1834)<br />
        → <b>Margaret Georgina Elizabeth Downing</b> (1803–1867)
      </p>
      <p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px"><b>Trick</b>, <b>Heard</b> and <b>Hooper</b> are
        distinctively <b>Devon</b> surnames. Untested — and note that Margaret's own 1867 death notice
        records her parents only as <b>"Both dead"</b>, naming neither.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Seven moves in seven generations</h2>
  <p class="dek">The whole route, end to end.</p>
  <p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:15px;line-height:2;margin-top:14px">
    <b>Württemberg</b> (1765) &rarr; <b>Soho and Bond Street, London</b> (1793–1819) &rarr;
    <b><em>Nautilus</em>, Gravesend</b> (3 Dec 1819) &rarr; <b>Algoa Bay</b> (14 Apr 1820) &rarr;
    <b>Uitenhage · Graaff-Reinet · Philippolis · Bethulie · Burgersdorp · Colesberg</b> (1822–1844) &rarr;
    <b>Barkly East</b> (1859–1907) &rarr; <b>Johannesburg</b> (1900s) &rarr; <b>Brisbane</b>
  </p>
</section>
